WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.960
If before it was in the realm of like 1-2 million downloads a month, these days it's already have like more than 40 million downloads a month.

00:06.960 --> 00:08.160
So it's quite significant.

00:08.160 --> 00:11.820
The main one, which is like the matrix against like all different variations.

00:11.820 --> 00:14.280
I'm just like trying to find it now.

00:14.280 --> 00:17.480
We are talking 73 different variations.

00:17.480 --> 00:24.980
Dragonfly flavor, therefore this command is implemented and these arguments are supported.

00:24.980 --> 00:28.300
Hello everyone and welcome to another episode of Behind the Comet.

00:28.300 --> 00:31.320
My guest today is Daniel Moran.

00:31.320 --> 00:40.660
He is a maintainer of FakeRedis, a pure Python implementation of the Redis API that began as a way to write tests without standing up a real server.

00:40.660 --> 00:47.880
And now works the drop-in replacement for both RedisPy and VolkiPy, pulling over 5 million downloads a month.

00:47.880 --> 00:54.040
Beyond FakeRedis, he maintains Django Task Scheduler, built a GitHub Actions plugin for JetBrains IDEs,

00:54.040 --> 01:00.380
and helps run Django Commons, a community organization for keeping Django packages healthy.

01:00.380 --> 01:01.760
Welcome, Daniel.

01:01.760 --> 01:02.800
Thank you.

01:02.800 --> 01:05.400
And thank you for the nice introduction.

01:05.400 --> 01:09.720
So let's start with what FakeRedis actually is.

01:09.720 --> 01:13.820
Can you tell us a little more about what it does and how it all started?

01:13.820 --> 01:30.180
So initially, like I was looking for, like I wanted to run tests, like I had my software using Redis and obviously I didn't want to use like the production Redis deployment.

01:30.180 --> 01:34.980
So I was looking for a way around it.

01:34.980 --> 01:43.260
I also, at the time, it was more difficult to bring up a container of Redis for testing purposes.

01:43.260 --> 01:49.820
These days, it's like much easier, but like at the time, it was like a bit more difficult.

01:49.820 --> 02:00.820
So I was looking for a way around it and I found FakeRedis, but it wasn't maintained and it wasn't like, I think it was out of date with the latest Python version.

02:00.820 --> 02:11.400
Therefore, I adopted it and just like, at first, I just made sure that it works with the latest environment so I can actually use it.

02:11.400 --> 02:18.740
The idea is you can run, you can use it as your Redis within your own Python process.

02:18.740 --> 02:20.260
It's not an additional process.

02:20.260 --> 02:21.960
It's not an additional container.

02:21.960 --> 02:28.520
It's just like you can use it as Redis within your Python single thread.

02:28.520 --> 02:29.260
Right.

02:29.260 --> 02:36.140
And there was a big jump in adoption when Reddit implemented those changes in regards to their license.

02:36.140 --> 02:38.120
So they changed their license.

02:38.120 --> 02:41.000
There was a backlash and then they evoked part of it back.

02:41.000 --> 02:48.020
So I'm wondering as a downstream maintainer, how closely you were watching that and how did it change?

02:48.020 --> 02:50.140
How did it impact fake Redis basically?

02:50.140 --> 03:02.140
It really, I think it kind of changed the main use case from if before it was for testing purposes, just being replacement to run within the Python process.

03:02.140 --> 03:15.800
I started getting issues of it, like it doesn't implement certain Valky commands and or it's like the Valky responses are different or Dragonfly have like a command, which is like not in Redis.

03:15.800 --> 03:17.620
So it's like, can it be implemented?

03:17.620 --> 03:25.420
If before it was in the realm of like one, two million downloads a month, these days it's already have like more than 40 million downloads a month.

03:25.420 --> 03:33.740
So it's quite significant and these days it actually has multiple flavors of running.

03:33.740 --> 03:39.120
Like while it's like the package is still called fake Redis, it implements like fake Valky.

03:39.120 --> 03:47.980
It's also, if you look at the connectivity package, RedisPy or ValkyPy, it also implements both of them.

03:47.980 --> 03:48.380
Right.

03:48.380 --> 03:52.660
So one use case is for writing tests, which is how it all initially started.

03:52.660 --> 03:59.240
And then you mentioned about the case of people wanting to switch from Redis to Valky, right?

03:59.240 --> 04:05.800
So this was the tool to make sure for people to know if actually their setup is agnostic, right?

04:05.800 --> 04:13.400
Is this still the main use case or are there any other use cases how people nowadays use fake Redis?

04:13.400 --> 04:22.660
So obviously, like I cannot really tell like in particular how they use it, but I just like based on the different issues that people are opening,

04:22.660 --> 04:29.180
I can tell that people use it to determine whether they can upgrade, which is also a use case.

04:29.180 --> 04:30.640
Like it's basically changing.

04:30.640 --> 04:33.060
Also, it's like it's similar to being agnostic.

04:33.060 --> 04:38.540
Like can I upgrade or am I using a command, a Redis command, which was deprecated?

04:38.540 --> 04:41.460
Or am I agnostic?

04:41.460 --> 04:43.120
Can I switch to Dragonfly?

04:43.120 --> 04:44.640
Can I switch to Valky?

04:44.640 --> 04:46.240
Can I switch back to Redis?

04:46.240 --> 04:48.320
And so on and so forth.

04:48.320 --> 04:48.740
Right.

04:48.740 --> 04:51.440
And you can also run fake Redis as a server, right?

04:51.440 --> 05:00.960
So yeah, this is like, I would say in the past year, I added the ability to run fake Redis as TCP server in its own thread.

05:00.960 --> 05:10.140
And it will respond just like you can, depending on what flavor you instantiate it on, it will respond like Valky, Redis, like Dragonfly.

05:10.140 --> 05:12.220
Obviously, it's implemented in Python.

05:12.220 --> 05:17.980
You shouldn't expect the same performance as Redis, Valky, Dragonfly.

05:17.980 --> 05:23.820
But it does save you the time of setting up the networking between containers and so on.

05:23.820 --> 05:32.620
So if we're looking at the project, which is not really about like the performance, then it is a good solution for that.

05:32.620 --> 05:44.020
So that means basically that if I want to run Redis, but actually I don't need Redis performance and I want something that's simpler and good enough for my use case,

05:44.020 --> 05:49.140
then I could use fake Redis instead of using Redis because it would be slower.

05:49.140 --> 05:52.940
But on the other hand, for some use cases, I guess slower is okay.

05:52.940 --> 05:54.600
Sometimes you just care about simplicity.

05:54.600 --> 05:57.600
You just want to set up something and you want it to work right.

05:57.600 --> 05:59.420
So this is the use case.

05:59.420 --> 06:04.560
You're saying that people use it mostly for simplicity instead of Redis when they use it locally.

06:04.560 --> 06:04.840
Yeah.

06:04.840 --> 06:15.120
And you can also argue that if your networking setup is not that fast, then actually, like, you know, the Python performance from compute perspective, it's slower.

06:15.120 --> 06:21.520
But if your networking is slow, then actually it's better because it works within the same Python process.

06:21.520 --> 06:21.920
Right.

06:21.920 --> 06:27.140
And you said that the goal for fake Redis is actually to be server agnostic.

06:27.140 --> 06:33.820
So people can use it to test, for example, if they can switch to Redis or Volkey or any of those.

06:33.820 --> 06:36.940
So I'm wondering, how does that actually work under the hood?

06:36.940 --> 06:40.480
So does it mean that features get ported command by command?

06:40.480 --> 06:42.320
Or do you have any other way?

06:42.320 --> 06:45.780
How do you make sure that actually it is server agnostic?

06:45.780 --> 06:51.920
Meanwhile, all those tools are adding new features and bug fixes into their packages.

06:51.920 --> 06:58.420
So I am closely watching the release notes of Redis, Volkey, Dragonfly.

06:58.420 --> 07:01.000
I am implementing changes.

07:01.000 --> 07:05.240
Also, when a command is deprecated, I make sure that it's deprecated.

07:05.240 --> 07:16.500
And one good example is Dragonfly implements a command set EX, which is set expire, which is not implemented in Redis and Volkey.

07:16.500 --> 07:23.100
So when you instantiate fake Redis with the Dragonfly flavor, the command will be supported.

07:23.100 --> 07:30.020
When you instantiate fake Redis with Redis or Volkey flavor, you will get an error, just like Redis or Volkey will give you.

07:30.020 --> 07:43.020
So the CI-CD that I have set up in GitHub Action, it's testing against like different variations, different Python version, different Redis Pi version and different Redis server versions.

07:43.020 --> 07:46.780
And also Volkey Pi and Volkey versions and Dragonfly as well.

07:46.780 --> 07:50.340
Now I'm wondering how does your test matrix look like?

07:50.340 --> 07:57.400
Because you said you're testing against different Python versions and against different Redis and Dragonfly versions.

07:57.400 --> 07:59.780
So how does the matrix look like?

07:59.780 --> 08:05.400
And how do you make sure it doesn't keep on exploding when there are all the time like new versions coming in?

08:05.400 --> 08:09.800
So yeah, the test matrix, I can share my screen if you want.

08:09.800 --> 08:11.000
It's like, yeah.

08:11.000 --> 08:13.800
How many jobs do you have?

08:13.800 --> 08:14.640
One second.

08:14.640 --> 08:18.640
Is it a few or dozens or hundreds?

08:18.640 --> 08:25.020
So I do have like a few, like whether it is like formatting, linting and stuff like this.

08:25.020 --> 08:29.960
But like the main one, which is like the matrix against like all different variations.

08:29.960 --> 08:32.740
I'm just like trying to find it now.

08:32.740 --> 08:36.540
We are talking 73 different variations.

08:36.540 --> 08:36.880
Oh.

08:36.880 --> 08:45.280
Different Redis version, Python version, Redis Pi version, and also like you have Valky or ValkyPy.

08:45.280 --> 08:46.060
Yeah.

08:46.060 --> 08:53.480
And you mentioned a case of a command, if I understood you correctly, that existed in Redis, but not in Dragonfly, right?

08:53.480 --> 08:54.600
The other way around.

08:54.600 --> 08:55.740
The other way around, right.

08:55.740 --> 09:04.460
So I'm wondering when you have this case where there are two compatible servers and they disagree that there is one feature in one and not in the other, etc.

09:04.460 --> 09:07.060
How does fake Redis handle that?

09:07.060 --> 09:09.980
How do you decide what is the right behavior?

09:09.980 --> 09:13.920
Does it mean that you have features from both or only from one or the other?

09:13.920 --> 09:15.460
Or what is your approach to it?

09:15.460 --> 09:38.080
So the way that I think that's the core of fake Redis, when you want to implement the command, you have the fake Redis framework, which just like as an abstraction layers, which hide like all the socket interface of like getting basically the command as on the network.

09:38.080 --> 09:52.360
And it just like parse all the different, the command and different arguments as part of the way, like when the command is parsed, it says like, okay, I'm a fake Redis with Dragonfly flavor.

09:52.360 --> 09:56.080
Therefore, this command is implemented and these arguments are supported.

09:56.080 --> 10:03.040
Or alternatively, it's like, it's going to say, oh, I am fake Redis with Redis flavor.

10:03.040 --> 10:05.880
And therefore this command is not supported through an error.

10:05.880 --> 10:06.880
Right.

10:06.880 --> 10:13.560
So that means that users always know what is the flavor and they can pick their own flavor, right?

10:13.560 --> 10:14.920
When they instantiate.

10:14.920 --> 10:15.620
Yeah, exactly.

10:15.620 --> 10:28.500
When, when they instantiate fake Redis, I mean, the default is a Redis 7, sorry, Redis 7, but they can instantiate like with any, any flavor of like Valky, Dragonfly or Redis.

10:28.500 --> 10:31.920
And Redis is supported for version 6, 7, and 8.

10:31.920 --> 10:42.440
So also if like a command was introduced, for example, in Redis 8 and it didn't exist in Redis 7, it's going to throw an error if you instantiate it with Redis 7 flavor.

10:42.440 --> 10:45.840
And fake Redis is a very popular package.

10:45.840 --> 10:53.420
So it has around 41 millions of downloads per month and 304 millions of downloads since you adopted it.

10:53.420 --> 10:57.400
And you mentioned to me that you do this in your free time.

10:57.400 --> 10:58.860
So this is sort of your hobby.

10:58.860 --> 11:02.320
This is not your main work where you're employed to maintain it.

11:02.320 --> 11:09.640
So I'm wondering what does keep you motivate to, what does motivate you to keep maintaining it?

11:09.640 --> 11:11.840
And what was your primarily motivation?

11:11.840 --> 11:14.500
Why did you get started?

11:14.500 --> 11:18.300
So initially, like I said, it's like I had this need.

11:18.300 --> 11:29.900
I just like I didn't, I was looking for a way to not deploy a Redis container on the side and then set up like the network in between like how I run my CI CD.

11:29.900 --> 11:34.160
So that was like why I adopted it in the first place.

11:34.160 --> 11:35.080
A personal need.

11:35.080 --> 11:42.820
These days, I think just like because it's so popular, first of all, it's like it's cool to see that people use what I'm doing.

11:42.820 --> 11:59.180
And there is the technical challenge of, I mean, if you look at the test matrix of like all the different variations that people are using it and like being able to support like all the different variations because it's so popular.

11:59.180 --> 12:05.400
And it's like if now I want to introduce something new, I need to consider like all the different variations when I consider it.

12:05.400 --> 12:08.240
So there is like the technical challenge.

12:08.240 --> 12:10.320
I enjoy the technical challenge.

12:10.320 --> 12:11.640
I'm learning a lot from this.

12:11.640 --> 12:24.000
Otherwise, I also I can say it's like I had like, for example, like in one of the job interviews that I had, the interviewer said like, oh, you're the maintainer of fake Redis.

12:24.000 --> 12:26.480
Like, so I don't need to test your technical abilities.

12:26.480 --> 12:28.340
Like, obviously, you know what you're doing.

12:28.340 --> 12:30.260
So there is a value in this.

12:30.260 --> 12:36.640
Like, while maybe people are more familiar with my GitHub handle because like this is where they downloaded it from.

12:36.640 --> 12:44.880
But once they realize that this connection, there is, it gives me a sense of like some kind of credentials, like that I know what I'm doing.

12:44.880 --> 12:50.060
And it's not, let's say I didn't get like how to implement the tree in my latest interviews.

12:50.060 --> 12:59.000
Other than this, I can say that also I had like two leads, two clients that I got thanks to fake Redis.

12:59.000 --> 13:00.280
They just like approached me.

13:00.280 --> 13:07.240
One of them actually like they just saw like what I did with GitHub Action, like implementing this massive matrix of like what's going on.

13:07.240 --> 13:12.580
And they say like, yeah, we need like to do something similar in our situation, like with GitHub Actions.

13:12.580 --> 13:16.960
And you obviously like you know how to create like this matrix in GitHub Actions.

13:16.960 --> 13:19.520
So yeah, and this I did the project for them.

13:19.520 --> 13:23.860
Another one they wanted to extend actually fake Redis.

13:23.860 --> 13:27.160
So and have it support something else.

13:27.160 --> 13:33.260
So overall, I would say, yes, it is technically challenging and I enjoy this.

13:33.260 --> 13:44.100
And yeah, it does like, while it doesn't pay the bills like directly, but yeah, the fact that then interviews, like it gives me some credibility before even being interviewed.

13:44.100 --> 13:46.740
And like it gets me leads and it is valuable.

13:46.740 --> 13:51.960
Right. So basically, you're saying that it's something you enjoy doing in your free time.

13:51.960 --> 13:59.060
But also it's something that shaped your career, which I think is very nice if you're able to combine both.

13:59.060 --> 14:03.460
So actually, your hobby can also be useful to you in your professional life.

14:03.460 --> 14:08.800
So fake Redis is maintained by in total one person.

14:08.800 --> 14:09.900
You, right?

14:09.900 --> 14:12.020
You have no other team members.

14:12.020 --> 14:13.860
So you are the team of one.

14:13.860 --> 14:23.660
So I'm wondering for someone starting a popular project or inheriting it, what actually works for avoiding burnout if you are the only maintainer there?

14:23.660 --> 14:31.260
Do you have any specific rules, for example, when it comes to interaction with people on the Internet or when it comes to yourself?

14:31.260 --> 14:39.420
How do you basically prevent yourself getting exhausted because of so many issues and pull requests incoming?

14:39.420 --> 14:46.300
So I am trying to, this is generally like also what I do with work.

14:46.300 --> 14:51.020
I'm trying to, there is like inbox zero is a way of life.

14:51.020 --> 14:53.520
So that way it's like I'm never overwhelmed.

14:53.520 --> 15:00.520
And also when it comes to fake Redis, I'm trying to, so the equivalent of like inbox zero, it's like list of issues zero.

15:00.520 --> 15:06.560
So I'm trying to address it as soon as possible and make sure that the list doesn't grow.

15:06.560 --> 15:20.760
If like I'm trying to be responsive to issues, if I need like additional details or something like this, I'm also encouraging people like, okay, I mean, you, you describe the issue, you know what it is.

15:20.760 --> 15:22.960
Do you mind actually implementing the code for it?

15:22.960 --> 15:27.140
And it's like, that's where I'm also encouraging people to contribute.

15:27.140 --> 15:34.360
And if needed, I just like give them a link to the relevant part of the, the commentation of like how to do it.

15:34.360 --> 15:35.800
I am doing it in my free time.

15:35.800 --> 15:39.600
So obviously I'm making sure like to keep my priorities straight.

15:39.600 --> 15:48.920
So yes, if now I don't see it happening, but like if in theory I wake up and I see that I have like a list of like 50 new issues, it never happened.

15:48.920 --> 15:54.560
But if in theory something like this will happen, then I will just like make sure that I have my priorities straight.

15:54.560 --> 16:00.320
And like, you know, I'm, yeah, it's important, like you said, to have a balance and not to get burned out.

16:00.320 --> 16:07.200
I'll do like an issue per day or maybe like two per day, see if I can merge some of them together.

16:07.200 --> 16:12.460
And I'm wondering, it's 2026 and I cannot not to mention AI.

16:12.460 --> 16:18.100
So it's everywhere nowadays and it's in open source projects too.

16:18.100 --> 16:25.320
So I'm wondering as a maintainer of a popular open source projects, what are your thoughts about AI?

16:25.320 --> 16:29.800
Is it good or bad thing?

16:29.800 --> 16:31.980
It's both.

16:31.980 --> 16:33.200
Of course.

16:33.200 --> 16:36.040
I mean, it's, I would say it like this.

16:36.040 --> 16:38.160
It's like, it's actually related also.

16:38.160 --> 16:44.500
Like I'll start with the bad and then I'll go with the good because that's kind of like as a developer, this is how I am, I guess.

16:44.500 --> 16:45.760
You mentioned the burned out.

16:45.760 --> 16:52.180
So it's actually very recently, like you have a lot of PRs that are obviously written by AI.

16:52.180 --> 16:59.400
And because it's the documentation is, well, I'm praising myself, but I do think the documentation of fake Redis is good.

16:59.400 --> 17:07.780
So is AI, like the code quality is, it's very good, I would say, but we're talking about edge cases.

17:07.780 --> 17:14.020
For example, like what happens if the user sends, this is one of the latest PR that I got.

17:14.020 --> 17:22.540
What happens when the user sends a negative amount for like the count, which I mean, okay, it is a valid edge case.

17:22.540 --> 17:29.580
Nobody cared about it for like the past five years that I've been maintaining this package.

17:29.580 --> 17:40.400
So you can argue that it's just like created an additional overhead for me to review the code and then to maintain the code because it's just like it's code that now it's never going to leave.

17:40.400 --> 17:44.300
And if nobody cared about it for five years, then should it be there?

17:44.300 --> 17:52.820
But then again, it is a valid case because this is like I want to be like as compatible as possible with Valky, with Redis and so on.

17:52.820 --> 17:54.700
So it is a valid case.

17:54.700 --> 18:06.000
So basically you're saying that it puts some like maintenance burden on you because you have to review it and you have to maintain it, but you are not really sure if that's like worth the effort, the value that it brings.

18:06.000 --> 18:09.740
Because it's an edge case that no one complains about, if I understand it correctly.

18:09.740 --> 18:10.480
Exactly.

18:10.480 --> 18:10.580
Yeah.

18:10.580 --> 18:11.060
Right.

18:11.060 --> 18:15.260
So like it basically creates a significant overhead.

18:15.260 --> 18:22.080
I did create like the CloudMD and I did, I'm not, English isn't my first language.

18:22.080 --> 18:29.640
So obviously like having AI, reviewing the documentation, creating the fake Redis logo and so on and so forth.

18:29.640 --> 18:30.560
Absolutely amazing.

18:30.560 --> 18:35.180
If now in the recent year, I implemented the Vector Set commands.

18:35.180 --> 18:38.780
Now, whenever implementing, they're implementing a new command.

18:38.780 --> 18:41.880
I have a very good documentation, how to do it.

18:41.880 --> 18:47.420
It's still a boilerplate code that needs to be written because it's documented properly.

18:47.420 --> 18:51.000
AI is doing it very, very well.

18:51.000 --> 18:56.620
And then it leaves me with like actually implementing the data structure for like the Vector Set commands.

18:56.620 --> 19:03.040
And it's, this is basically, it takes away all the nitty gritty kind of boring work.

19:03.040 --> 19:04.380
It takes it away.

19:04.380 --> 19:08.320
And so on this side, I think AI is absolutely amazing.

19:08.320 --> 19:18.800
It's, I would say it would have taken me probably double the time to implement the Vector Set commands if it wasn't for the AI.

19:18.800 --> 19:19.080
Right.

19:19.080 --> 19:20.000
All right.

19:20.000 --> 19:22.900
So it's a useful tool that you use in your daily life.

19:22.900 --> 19:24.180
It makes you more productive.

19:24.180 --> 19:30.040
But also sometimes there's this burden of people misusing it and just sending random pull requests.

19:30.040 --> 19:33.900
I'm wondering how often do you have to deal with those random pull requests?

19:33.900 --> 19:38.000
Is this something that happens every day or once a week or once a month?

19:38.000 --> 19:44.360
So actually recently in the past month, there was quite a lot of them.

19:44.360 --> 19:56.240
I noticed that when I see multiple pull requests by the same user, I'm also looking at the user and I see that I'm not sure what's the motivation behind it.

19:56.240 --> 20:05.300
But if I see a user that is contributing to 100 packages in a week, basically having AI running and just like finding bugs and fixing them.

20:05.300 --> 20:09.440
It's like, I'm not sure what's the motivation behind it.

20:09.440 --> 20:16.280
I'm kind of, it's obviously like it creates an additional overhead and I try to discourage it.

20:16.280 --> 20:26.040
I'm saying like, you know, hey, like merge all of your PRs into one and I'll review it all at once instead of like sending every time that I close one.

20:26.040 --> 20:28.040
It's like the day after I'm getting an additional one.

20:28.040 --> 20:42.900
So, yes, I am encouraging contribution, but it's important that the contribution has value, whether it is to other people that want to contribute or to the users of fake Redis as well.

20:42.900 --> 20:43.420
Right.

20:43.420 --> 20:46.000
Because of the maintenance burden, basically.

20:46.000 --> 20:53.860
Speaking of it, so you also, except of fake Redis, you also created the GitHub actions plugin for JetBrains IDE.

20:53.860 --> 21:01.580
This is a plugin that started as open source and then you decided to monetize it because of the maintenance burden that you had with that.

21:01.580 --> 21:05.820
So, could you tell us a little bit more about how it started and what does it do?

21:05.820 --> 21:19.920
So, I was actually working for a client which had, I think it was like a project with 40 repositories and jumping from IntelliJ, like JetBrains IDE, IntelliJ, PyChamp, whatever.

21:19.920 --> 21:26.460
It's like jumping from IntelliJ to the browser to IntelliJ to the browser and having like 40 tabs open.

21:26.460 --> 21:35.220
I, basically, it drove me a bit crazy and I said like, why isn't it in the IDE as a, like, I mean, I have Git.

21:35.220 --> 21:36.860
Why don't I have GitHub actions?

21:36.860 --> 21:39.820
Like, I have, I have a terminal in the IDE.

21:39.820 --> 21:41.680
I have like connection to database.

21:41.680 --> 21:43.340
Like, this is, what is an IDE?

21:43.340 --> 21:45.200
It's an integrated development environment.

21:45.200 --> 21:47.240
You want it integrated with GitHub actions as well.

21:47.240 --> 21:49.140
It's like, it's almost a missing piece.

21:49.140 --> 21:49.920
It wasn't there.

21:49.920 --> 22:00.020
And, yeah, I just, like, I implemented it just so, basically, you don't have to jump to the browser to see GitHub actions, all the workflows and all the logs.

22:00.020 --> 22:03.080
It was open source at the beginning, like you said.

22:03.080 --> 22:10.100
Maintaining a plugin for JetBrains with SDK, they introduced, like, breaking changes, like, once a year.

22:10.100 --> 22:12.020
And it is quite difficult.

22:12.020 --> 22:20.760
And people, obviously, like, yes, I think it's, like, it reached at the time to 100,000 downloads when it was still open source.

22:20.760 --> 22:29.360
So, obviously, like, people liked it, started asking for different features, like, okay, why can I just, like, you know, trigger a workflow?

22:29.360 --> 22:32.740
Can I, like, filtering of, like, different workflows and so on and so forth.

22:32.740 --> 22:34.720
And at some point I said, like, okay, I'm in.

22:34.720 --> 22:35.260
I'm sorry.

22:35.260 --> 22:44.320
It's, like, the original feature of seeing a list of workflow runs, list of jobs for workflow runs and the logs.

22:44.320 --> 22:51.020
This is still free, but all the additional features in these days, there is a list.

22:51.020 --> 22:56.300
I mean, people can go and have a look at, like, the list of, like, different features that exist there.

22:56.300 --> 22:58.900
But all these additional features are actually paid.

22:58.900 --> 23:01.760
And, yeah, it's this subscription base.

23:01.760 --> 23:07.140
And also, I make sure that it's up-to-date and supports the latest JetBrains IDs.

23:07.140 --> 23:11.300
And so, there are some features that are still for free, right?

23:11.300 --> 23:12.620
And some of them are paid.

23:12.620 --> 23:16.140
So, I'm wondering, how did you split them?

23:16.140 --> 23:28.080
So, the original features of, like, my original problem was I don't want to jump to, from IDE to browser to view workflow runs.

23:28.080 --> 23:35.820
And then, like, when clicking on workflow run, seeing the list of jobs and then per job, like, to view the logs.

23:35.820 --> 23:36.080
Yeah.

23:36.080 --> 23:40.800
So, this is something which you basically needed three tabs to do what I just said.

23:40.800 --> 23:48.580
Like, one for the list of workflow runs, one for the list of jobs in the workflow run, and one for the logs of a single job.

23:48.580 --> 23:51.380
And multiply this by multiple repositories.

23:51.380 --> 23:54.740
So, it's, like, the number of tabs is just it grows exponentially.

23:54.740 --> 23:56.160
At some point, it's, like, it's ridiculous.

23:56.160 --> 24:04.260
This original feature, which I was aiming to solve, and I solved it in the plugin, these are still free.

24:04.260 --> 24:10.740
And I aim to keep it free unless JetBrains are going to make it very difficult to maintain it.

24:10.740 --> 24:12.820
I'm planning to keep it free forever.

24:12.820 --> 24:25.940
All the additional features that, like, just, like, got added with time, these are just, like, with the premium version that you need to pay for.

24:25.940 --> 24:28.800
I do have, these days, 700,000 downloads.

24:28.800 --> 24:33.020
Yeah, obviously, people like it, and the majority are using the free version.

24:33.020 --> 24:33.780
They're not paying.

24:33.780 --> 24:34.360
I'm not.

24:34.360 --> 24:38.820
It would have been nice to get, like, $2 a month from, like, 700,000 downloads.

24:38.820 --> 24:51.000
Yeah, and so, except the fake redis and the GitHub's plugin, you're also involved in the Django Commons project, right?

24:51.000 --> 24:56.920
So, could you tell us a little bit more about what it is?

24:56.920 --> 25:06.640
So, Django Commons, it is an organization that aims to support maintainers of Django packages.

25:06.640 --> 25:08.360
It's similar to JazzBem.

25:08.360 --> 25:20.720
The idea is to support maintainers, improve, like, the experience of, like, maintaining Django packages, whether it is, like, for the original maintainer or for contributors.

25:20.720 --> 25:30.180
Whether it is, like, to reduce the barrier to entry for new contributors, just make it easy, guiding them, documentation, explaining the best practices.

25:30.180 --> 25:50.740
And reducing some overhead for, like, package maintainers, showing, again, best practices, package, addressing security issue, having a community support where we kind of listen to what their needs are and see if we can address it in the organization level.

25:50.740 --> 25:56.800
And, you know, if one package has the problem, chances are that other packages are having similar problem.

25:56.800 --> 26:06.300
And if one package is solved, then chances are, like, that this solution can be applied to other packages in some 80% similar to solution, I would say.

26:06.300 --> 26:11.600
So, it's about sharing best practices and supporting each other, basically.

26:11.600 --> 26:12.620
Yes.

26:12.620 --> 26:20.280
And if someone would like to start contributing to fake redis tomorrow or maybe to learn a little bit more about it, where should they begin?

26:20.280 --> 26:22.980
Because you mentioned that fake redis has a really good documentation.

26:22.980 --> 26:27.380
So, is this the right place where people should start?

26:27.380 --> 26:30.580
Or is there anything else you would like to mention?

26:30.580 --> 26:38.800
I think, like, yeah, the documentation and the GitHub repository where you can see, like, issues in the documentation.

26:38.800 --> 26:44.080
Also, you can see the commands that are implemented and, obviously, like, the commands that are not implemented.

26:44.080 --> 26:48.080
So, this is, like, if you need an idea of, like, where to go first.

26:48.080 --> 26:53.440
Obviously, if you see in the documentation something which is not clear, I think the documentation are clear.

26:53.440 --> 26:57.980
But, I mean, the fact that it's clear to me doesn't mean that it is clear.

26:57.980 --> 27:05.080
So, if, like, obviously, like, I think this is just as valid contribution, if not even more important, to improve the documentation constantly.

27:05.080 --> 27:07.200
Hmm.

27:07.200 --> 27:10.560
And where would you want all of this to go next?

27:10.560 --> 27:17.480
Is there any version of done for a project like fake redis or any big vision you have?

27:17.480 --> 27:19.840
Or where do you see it in five or ten years?

27:19.840 --> 27:27.620
I would say that in my vision, I would say, is to have, like, fake redis becoming the standard for the protocol.

27:27.620 --> 27:33.540
Because Valkyve forked from, I think it was redis 7.2, if I remember correctly.

27:33.540 --> 27:37.640
And ever since, you can tell that, like, they already started diverging.

27:37.640 --> 27:41.680
And you see there are commands that are implemented in one and not the other.

27:41.680 --> 27:44.760
Dragonfly was already, like, having their own path.

27:44.760 --> 27:49.280
And, yes, they are, like, aligning with, like, the standard, but not 100%.

27:49.280 --> 27:51.000
So, there is no standard.

27:51.000 --> 27:54.160
Each implementation can do what they want.

27:54.160 --> 27:55.360
They can introduce new commands.

27:55.360 --> 27:59.600
They can introduce, like, how the response should look like.

27:59.600 --> 28:05.960
Sometimes you have examples where in some commands, like, dragonfly returns null while this returns zero.

28:05.960 --> 28:07.060
There is no right or wrong.

28:07.060 --> 28:11.060
It's just, like, different implementations.

28:11.060 --> 28:20.720
And because there is no standard, you cannot really know what to expect in certain situations, especially if you want to be agnostic.

28:20.720 --> 28:22.440
Fake redis allows you to be agnostic.

28:22.440 --> 28:31.720
And I do, if I'm looking at the term, in terms of, like, in 5, 10 years, I would love it if fake redis would be, in a way, the standard.

28:31.720 --> 28:40.980
So, if redis, dragonfly, or any other implementation that comes, like, would like to implement a new, introduce a new command, it will be, like, introduce a standard.

28:40.980 --> 28:44.460
And then others can implement this standard.

28:44.460 --> 28:52.700
That way, you can have, like, also one connectivity package instead of, like, having different ones for each implementation.

28:52.700 --> 28:57.260
Because eventually, they are, I mean, Valky is a fork of a certain version of redis.

28:57.260 --> 28:59.940
So, obviously, they are similar, very similar.

28:59.940 --> 29:01.500
You can say, like, 95%.

29:01.500 --> 29:08.120
But with time, at least the way it goes at the moment, they're going to diverge more and more.

29:08.120 --> 29:08.600
Right.

29:08.600 --> 29:14.780
So, the big vision of fake redis is becoming the standard in the ecosystem.

29:14.780 --> 29:15.160
Hopefully.

29:15.160 --> 29:23.260
And the last question for me is, if people would like to get in touch with you or follow you on social media, where can they find you?

29:23.260 --> 29:29.240
So, I would assume, like, you can put, like, obviously, they can go, like, to my GitHub profile.

29:29.240 --> 29:32.940
And, like, there you have a link to my LinkedIn and my email.

29:32.940 --> 29:35.960
And I would assume you can put, like, a link to my GitHub profile.

29:35.960 --> 29:40.340
Yes, I will put all links in the description so you can also find it there.

29:40.340 --> 29:44.600
So, yeah, like, in my LinkedIn and GitHub, that's, like, probably the best way to approach me.

29:44.600 --> 29:46.420
Thank you very much for coming.

29:46.420 --> 29:47.200
Thank you, Mia.

29:47.200 --> 29:48.120
I really appreciate it.
